Shared pieces: `styles.css` (design system), `site.js` (nav, version tags, supporters), `download-links.js`, `changelog.js`, `home.js`, `faq.js`. The header and footer markup is repeated in every page, so nav changes need to be made in each HTML file.

## Updating releases

Versions, download URLs and changelogs are driven by plain text files. No HTML edits are required for a release:

1. Set the version in `public.version` or `beta.version`
2. Keep URL templates in `win.pub` / `mac.pub` / `linux.pub` (and `*.beta`) using `{public.version}` or `{beta.version}`
3. Update `public.change` / `beta.change`: a bare version line (e.g. `3.0.1`) starts a release, and each following line is one change. `Title — description` (or `Title: description`) renders the title in bold.

What reads them:

- Every element with `data-version-of="public|beta"` (nav button, footer, hero pill, download pages) shows the current version.
- Download buttons (`data-download="win|mac|linux"`) get their URLs from the templates, and the file name under each button updates too. The homepage hero button picks the visitor's OS automatically.
- `public.html` / `beta.html` render the full changelog as a release timeline. The homepage "What's new" section shows the first release in `public.change`.

Download buttons and version tags include fallback values so links still work if JavaScript fails. After a bump, those fallbacks in `index.html`, `public.html` and `beta.html` will be one version behind until updated. That's harmless, because JS replaces them on load.

## Local preview

This is a static site. Open any HTML file in a browser, or serve the folder:

```bash
npx serve .
```

## Deploy

Pushes to `main` deploy via GitHub Pages (`.github/workflows/static.yml`). Custom domain is configured in `CNAME`.
